{"description":"Jane Fonda playfully teases Barbra Streisand for delivering a tribute to Robert Redford at the 2026 Oscars, while recalling her own multiple collaborations with the late actor.","articleSummary":"During the 98th Academy Awards in March 2026, Jane Fonda appeared on the Vanity Fair Oscar Party’s red‑carpet and in an Entertainment Tonight interview to humorously remark that she had worked with the late Robert Redford in four films, unlike Barbra Streisand’s single collaboration, and that she "has more to say." Streisand had taken the stage during the In Memoriam segment to honor Redford, performing a portion of "The Way We Were" and speaking warmly about his courage, activism, and influence on independent filmmaking.

The article recounts Redford’s death in September 2025 at age 89, noting his roles in films such as The Chase (1966), Barefoot in the Park (1967), The Electric Horseman (1979) and Our Souls at Night (2017) – all of which featured Fonda. It highlights Fonda’s emotional tribute on Instagram, her recollection of Redford’s sense of humor and his impact on film and social issues, as well as Streisand’s own Instagram post praising his charisma and friendship.

Both actresses reflected on their shared history, with Fonda noting Redford’s work on the Sundance Institute and his advocacy for freedom of the press and the environment, while Streisand spoke of their opposite personalities during The Way We Were set and the mutual respect that developed between them. The piece concludes by situating the tribute within the broader context of the 98th Academy Awards hosted by Conan O’Brien at the Dolby Theatre.","points":["Jane Fonda quipped she worked with Robert Redford in four films versus Streisand’s single collaboration.","Barbra Streisand performed "The Way We Were" and spoke about Redford’s courage, activism, and influence on independent cinema during the Oscars In Memoriam segment.","Robert Redford died September 2025 at age 89; he appeared in The Chase (1966), Barefoot in the Park (1967), The Electric Horseman (1979), and Our Souls at Night (2017).","Fonda highlighted Redford’s role in advancing independent film and his defense of freedom of the press, environmental protection, and encouragement of new voices at the Sundance Institute.","Both Fonda and Streisand posted tributes on Instagram after Redford’s death, expressing sorrow and recalling their collaborative experiences."],"biasSummary":"The article adopts a warm, celebratory tone toward Robert Redford, emphasizing his activism, artistic influence, and personal friendship with the actresses. It contains no overt partisan language or critical analysis, but it leans toward affectionate remembrance and highlights only positive aspects of Redford’s legacy."}
